-- title:
--   The step index of a point in a lattice tile is that tile's lattice point
-- statement:
--   Fix a mesh $h > 0$. Let
--
--   $$h\mathbb{Z}^d \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \text{for every } i \text{ there is } n \in \mathbb{Z}
--   \text{ with } x_i = n h \,\} \qquad (\texttt{scaledLattice d h}),$$
--
--   and recall that the half-closed cubes
--
--   $$\tilde{A}_h(x) \;=\; \prod_i \big[\, x_i - h/2,\ x_i + h/2 \,\big) \qquad (\texttt{halfClosedCube h x}),$$
--
--   indexed by the points $x$ of $h\mathbb{Z}^d$, tile $\mathbb{R}^d$: every point of $\mathbb{R}^d$
--   lies in exactly one of them. The **step index** is
--
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; \big(\operatorname{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\big)_i \qquad (\texttt{stepIndex d h s}),$$
--
--   the nearest point of $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ coordinate by coordinate, which is the lattice point of the
--   tile containing $s$.
--
--   Let $x \in h\mathbb{Z}^d$ and let $s \in \tilde{A}_h(x)$. Then
--
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; x .$$
--
--   This is the defining characterisation of the step index, and the form in which it is used: whenever
--   a point is known to lie in a specific tile, the index function can be replaced by that tile's
--   lattice point. Together with the fact that the step index always lands in the scaled lattice, it
--   identifies $x_h$ as the projection associated with the tiling.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Strict positivity of the mesh is required, since the half-closed cubes tile
--   $\mathbb{R}^d$ only for $h > 0$.
